  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'rschema_0067' contains three tables: 'Customer', 'Rental_record', and 'Credit_change_record'.\nThe 'Customer' table represents customers of a rental service. It has four columns: 'Credit_score' (an integer), 'Customer_ID' (an integer, acting as the primary key), 'Name' (a variable character field of maximum length 255), and 'Phone' (another variable character field of maximum length 255).\nThe 'Rental_record' table records the rental activities of customers. It has five columns: 'Customer_ID' (an integer, acting as a foreign key referencing the 'Customer' table), 'Record_ID' (an integer, acting as the primary key), 'Rental_fee' (a float), 'Rental_time' (a datetime), and 'Return_time' (a datetime).\nThe 'Credit_change_record' table keeps track of credit score changes for customers. It has six columns: 'Change_reason' (a variable character field of maximum length 255), 'Change_time' (a datetime), 'Customer_ID' (an integer, acting as a foreign key referencing the 'Customer' table), 'New_credit_value' (a float), 'Old_credit_value' (a float), and 'Record_ID' (an integer, acting as the primary key).\nThese tables are related to each other through foreign keys, ensuring referential integrity. The schema is exhaustive and covers all the necessary aspects of the data model.",
  "gold_question": "A rental company needs to manage customer credit records. Customers have name, phone number, and credit score. Each rental needs to record rental time, return time, and rental fee. The company needs to track changes in customer credit, with each record including change time, change reason, old credit value, and new credit value, to facilitate credit assessment and performance evaluation.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0067.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0067\nCREATE TABLE `Customer` (\n  `Customer_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `Phone` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Credit_score` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Customer_ID`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Rental_record` (\n  `Record_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Customer_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Rental_fee` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Rental_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Return_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Record_ID`),\n  CONSTRAINT `Rental_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`Customer_ID`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`Customer_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Credit_change_record` (\n  `Record_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Customer_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Change_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Change_reason` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Old_credit_value` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `New_credit_value` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Record_ID`),\n  CONSTRAINT `Credit_change_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`Customer_ID`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`Customer_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
